# {{PROJECT_NAME}} environment template — copy to `.env` (repo root) for local dev.
#
# THE ENVIRONMENT MODEL (one codebase, env-selected):
#   local dev    → this file's values, filled in.
#   production   → NO .env file: the platform supplies runtime env vars and
#                  build args. Dev and prod MUST be different databases.
#
# KEEP THIS FILE AT THE REPO ROOT, and the real `.env` too. Two near-misses
# that fail silently in a monorepo:
#   - Naming it `.env.local` — a bundler may read that while the server's env
#     loader does not, so the client gets its keys and the server dies.
#   - Also creating `<app>/.env` — most loaders never overwrite an already-set
#     variable, so the inner file silently wins for that app while the rest of
#     the tree reads the root. Two halves of the app then point at different
#     databases.
#
# `.env` is gitignored via `.env*` with a single `!.env.example` exception, so
# hand-made copies (`.env.bak`, `.env.prod`) cannot be committed by accident.
#
# Every variable the project reads belongs here with a comment. The app should
# validate the whole set at boot and fail loudly naming the missing key rather
# than starting half-configured.

# ── Runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TEMPLATE: the database connection string, if any. Example:
# DATABASE_URL=

# Isolated database for the test suite. The test setup uses this INSTEAD of
# DATABASE_URL and should refuse to run the database suites against the shared
# dev database without it — fixtures on the dev database corrupt the world you
# develop in, and a running dev server makes the suites flake. Same connection
# string as DATABASE_URL with only the database name swapped; the name must
# contain "test".
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# Do NOT set NODE_ENV (or your stack's equivalent) here. Bundlers honor an
# env-file value at BUILD time, so a value here can silently ship a
# development build from a local `build` command.

# TEMPLATE: port and host for the local server. Agent sessions override PORT
# in their worktree's .env to stay off the owner's reserved ports
# ({{OWNER_PORTS}}).
# PORT=
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# ── Build-time (public by design, inlined into the client bundle) ───────────
# TEMPLATE: publishable keys only. Anything secret never goes in this section.
